## A clopen frontier at infinity

The lower finite region consists of surreals below at least one natural number. It is the union of the open rays $(-\infty,n)$, and its complement is open because a unit step below an infinitely large point remains infinitely large. Thus the region is clopen and disconnects the line.

<details><summary>Reveal the closedness argument</summary>
If $x$ is not below any natural number, then $x\ge n$ for every natural $n$. If $y>x-1$ and $y<n$, then $x<y+1<n+1$, a contradiction. Hence $(x-1,\infty)$ stays entirely outside the lower finite region.
</details>

